w20-fidelity-audit
------------------

This directory is a clean-room Python 3 standard-library method sketch. It is
original work for this repository and contains no third-party code.

Paper (method idea credited)
----------------------------
György Eigner, Patrik P. Süli and Roland Hollós.
"Trust, but verify, audit and attribute: a human-in-the-loop framework for
checking AI-produced research results". arXiv:2611.13042, v1, submitted
9 Nov 2026.
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13042
Licence: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0),
http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. No code is linked from the abs
page and none is used.

Method only, in this directory's own words: a tool pass vouches for the one
artefact it ran on; whether that artefact says what the claim says is a
separate human record; a person makes the final call at a set point in
time; a claim with two meanings is cut into readings on purpose; and every
step is credited to a person, a model or a tool. The paper's tool layer is
not used; two toy integer-identity tools stand in.

What is and is not taken from the paper: the title above is cited by name.
No other text from the paper is copied into this directory: README.md, this
NOTICE and the code paraphrase the method, and no sentence or phrase of the
abstract is reproduced (a test compares every run of five words against the
abs-page copy when that copy is present). No figures, tables or data are
copied; the PDF was not read. The paper's number tokens and its example are
recorded only in paper_evidence.json, as tokens, offsets and span hashes
(no span text), and are marked UNVERIFIED.

